00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:19.100
 But we should get going with just a discussion of what is, because the first thing is really, you know, what is TRV versus other forms of remote viewing?

00:16:19.100 --> 00:16:24.100
 Like, you know, where did it come from and what differentiates it from other forms of remote viewing?

00:16:24.100 --> 00:16:28.100
 I know you're trained in TRV with Ed Dame, so I'm sure you can answer that.

00:16:28.100 --> 00:16:29.100
 Yeah.

00:16:29.100 --> 00:16:37.100
 So, essentially, the original protocols of CRV were developed by Hal Putoff, Ingo Swann, and Russell Targ.

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:45.100
 Oh, man, I want to say late '60s, like '68, '72, when they actually had started, gotten things together.

00:16:45.100 --> 00:16:53.100
 I couldn't tell you what the actual project moniker was at the time, because there was five or six, I think it was eight, different.

00:16:53.100 --> 00:17:11.100
 Monikers for different projects doing different things, depending on whether they were with Stanford Research International, or whether or not they were with the Defense Intelligence Agency, or CIA at that point, because it kind of jumped back and forth.

00:17:11.100 --> 00:17:29.100
 And ultimately, it is a protocol that essentially, where one is sitting down and being passive in some nature, and you have a highly attention focus on whatever it is that you're going to target.

00:17:29.100 --> 00:17:39.100
 And then usually, almost 100% of the time, at least we prefer it this way, you're working at least single blind from the target, meaning you have no idea what you're working on.

00:17:39.100 --> 00:17:45.100
 Double blind would mean the person that gave you the set of target numbers also doesn't know what the target is.

00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:50.100
 Double blind works well for controlled scientific studies.

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:54.100
 It's used a lot when dealing with admitting a new drug to the FDA, so to speak.

00:17:54.100 --> 00:18:13.100
 It's a scientific, I'm going to say protocol, to ensure that the results are clean and free from interference, whether it's from the organizer of the project, or in our case, the project manager of the project.

00:18:13.100 --> 00:18:30.100
 And the individual viewer, so TRV is a derivative of the original CRV, or coordinate remote viewing protocols developed by SRI with Ingo Swann and the same group of folks.

00:18:30.100 --> 00:18:43.100
 They call it coordinate remote viewing because of the way in which they discovered that they could use association of the actual coordinates of a location on the planet and still get

00:18:43.100 --> 00:18:48.100
 remote viewers on target and get valid data.

00:18:48.100 --> 00:18:57.100
 It is more of an experiential, more active than passive, I would say, personally.

00:18:57.100 --> 00:19:08.100
 Modality where in which someone is actually, you know, let's say teasing their five senses in which to describe a target.

00:19:08.100 --> 00:19:22.100
 And TRV or technical remote viewing is an abstract of that or a reorganization of those stages or protocol that CRV is in itself.

00:19:22.100 --> 00:19:29.100
 CRV is the longest standing and most studied version of remote viewing.

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:45.100
 In the technical remote viewing which was coined technical remote viewing by Ed Dames, my guess is somewhere in the late 80s, mid 80s, he started working on what he perceived as improvements to the process of coordinate remote viewing.

00:19:45.100 --> 00:19:53.100
 Ed was personally trained up to, I believe it was stage three of the original CRV protocols, and even to this day, he will tell you that he's not that great a remote viewer.

00:19:53.100 --> 00:20:07.100
 And Morgan and I were kind of having a conversation about this last night that there are many people that say like to play football and aren't really good at playing football, but it doesn't mean they're not a very good coach, or at least understand the game in itself.

00:20:07.100 --> 00:20:21.100
 And that's kind of what we would describe at Dames as, at least in his own terms of not being a very good CRV remote viewer.

00:20:21.100 --> 00:20:27.100
 The protocols, over time they've changed.

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:39.100
 His original TRV with what he owned as Cytec in the mid 90s, after he got out of the, I believe it was still Army at that point.

00:20:39.100 --> 00:20:57.100
 And when he got out, he started to employ the other viewers from the program in a private business and started working corporate contracts and government contracts, working on various discrete targets for other people.

00:20:57.100 --> 00:21:11.100
 So while he was doing that, he was, he had already at this point had, I guess, really game plan and wondered and implemented some changes in how he thought he could make the process better.

00:21:11.100 --> 00:21:23.100
 In CRV you're running through a stage one, which is the diagram deployment and determining of archetypes or gestalts to the session.

00:21:23.100 --> 00:21:39.100
 Sometimes there's multiple archetypes and then you proceed into a process in stage two, where you are trying to experience and describe the target at its base level using your five senses.

00:21:39.100 --> 00:21:45.100
 So usually it starts out with colors, then, then feel or touch.

00:21:45.100 --> 00:21:46.100
 What does it feel like?

00:21:46.100 --> 00:21:47.100
 What is its density?

00:21:47.100 --> 00:21:49.100
 How does it feel sort of good?

00:21:49.100 --> 00:21:59.100
 And this is TRV, by the way, at this point where he's describing the particular senses to what it is that you were trying to describe.

00:21:59.100 --> 00:22:13.100
 So sight, touch, sound, taste, temperature, and visual dimensions is what originally, not originally, it's still both the same in CRV and TRV when you get past stage one for the most part.

00:22:13.100 --> 00:22:24.100
 But in CRV, you're running multiple pages depending on this free flowing, almost data stream of experiential data that you're getting from the target.

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:30.100
 And then you start moving into stage three, where in which you're actually sketching or trying to sketch.

00:22:30.100 --> 00:22:48.100
 And in CRV protocol, you're doing multiple pages of this until ultimately you arrive at this, I would say somewhat refined sketch, you're doing this sketch multiple times and trying to understand what it is that you're you're describing.

00:22:48.100 --> 00:22:51.100
 And then you move into a stage four matrix.

00:22:51.100 --> 00:23:17.100
 And again, you you're dealing with a stream of consciousness slash experiential data in that we're also you're describing sensory data like your five senses, dimensional data, as well as like the visual, the densities, the emotions, the curvings, and the other types of construction of the target, where in which it's more visual in that sense.

00:23:17.100 --> 00:23:26.100
 And then you're working into an emotional aesthetic impact, which is mostly the viewers emotional impact from the site, how they feel about the site.

00:23:26.100 --> 00:23:35.100
 Then there's any emotional impacts that the site might have at the site itself say you're describing.

00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:43.100
 This is kind of macabre but say you're you're describing Dachau camp circa 1943 or something like that there's going to be a lot of negative emotional impacts at the site.

00:23:43.100 --> 00:23:49.100
 You can at times hone in on particular people at times on what they're feeling.

00:23:49.100 --> 00:23:52.100
 That's the general idea of what's going on in this column.

00:23:52.100 --> 00:23:57.100
 And there's one, two, three, four, five, six, six columns.

00:23:57.100 --> 00:23:59.100
 I think it's six.

00:23:59.100 --> 00:24:04.100
 After that, then you're working, then you're working with tangible ideas and then intangible ideas.

00:24:04.100 --> 00:24:07.100
 And then there is another column for.

00:24:07.100 --> 00:24:10.100
 What is described as analytical overlay at that point.

00:24:10.100 --> 00:24:24.100
 And the analytical overlay is where in which a situation where in which you have data, but your lexicon of knowledge is not exactly able to describe in great detail what the concept is that's coming across.

00:24:24.100 --> 00:24:29.100
 Where in which you're using metaphor or analogy to describe the target.

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:40.100
 Now, there are some schools of thought where people don't rely upon analytical overlay in this particular stage, some people that do.

00:24:40.100 --> 00:24:53.100
 And you're getting another page of data about the sketches that you've gotten before it that based off of the sensor information that you're getting in stage two, and then your gestalt information that you're getting in stage one when it comes to the CRV protocol.

00:24:53.100 --> 00:25:05.100
 And at that point, you're, you've got multiple different pages of your stage two is multiple different pages of stage three is multiple different pages of stage fours, depending how large the gestalt is for your, your target.

00:25:05.100 --> 00:25:11.100
 And what CRV is differently is the reorganization of the stages.

00:25:11.100 --> 00:25:22.100
 Now, not necessarily out of order, because you're still building a sensory level of structure and building up to a more complex idea by the end of your session.

00:25:22.100 --> 00:25:23.100
